       "code": "a0, b0 = 1, 1                        # a flat prior: every rate equally plausible before seeing data\nk, n = 7, 10\na, b = a0 + k, b0 + n - k\npost = stats.beta(a, b)\nprint((a, b), round(post.mean(), 3), [round(float(x), 3) for x in post.interval(0.95)])\nprint(\"plain proportion\", k / n)",
       "say": "**Beta-binomial updating.** A belief about a rate between 0 and 1 is written as a **Beta(a, b)** curve. Start with Beta(1, 1), flat: \"any rate is equally plausible\". Each success adds 1 to a and each failure adds 1 to b: after 7 successes in 10 the belief is **Beta(8, 4)**, with a mean of **0.667** and a 95% credible interval of **0.39 to 0.89**. This reads the way people want it to: \"given the data and the starting belief, there is a 95% chance the rate lies between 39% and 89%\". The interval is wide, correctly: ten users do not pin the rate down. The plain proportion (0.7) is just the most likely value, and says nothing about how sure we are.",
