       "say": "**Comparing two options.** Design A: 120 conversions in 1,000; design B: 145 in 1,000. Draw many rates from each belief and compare: B is better in **94.9%** of the draws; the difference B − A has a mean of 0.0249 and a 95% credible interval of **−0.005 to +0.055**. The expected **loss** (how much conversion rate you give up, on average, if you choose wrongly) is 0.00033 if you choose B and 0.0252 if you choose A. \"B is probably better, and choosing it risks very little\" is a decision-ready statement, more useful than \"p = 0.07\". It also lets the decision depend on cost: if switching is expensive you may want a higher probability; if it is free, 95% is plenty.",
