   "simple": "A model predicts a house price of 300,000. How wrong could it be? **Conformal prediction** answers with an interval that comes with a **guarantee**: with data of the same kind, it contains the true value in (at least) 90% of cases, whatever the model is, however badly it is built. The recipe is short: hold out a **calibration set**, measure how wrong the model was there, take the error that 90% of the cases stayed below, and add and subtract it. The guarantee needs one assumption (new cases are like the calibration cases), and the lesson ends by showing what happens when that fails. The data are simulated: a straight-line relationship whose noise grows with x, which is a deliberately hard case.",
