       "say": "**Small calibration sets and the correction.** With only 10 calibration points, using the plain 90% quantile of the scores covers just **81.6%** on average, and with 30 points 86.8%: too few points are not enough to see the tail of the errors. The **(n + 1) / n correction** (use the quantile at level 0.9 × (1 + 1/n) instead) fixes the average: 91.0% and 90.5% for 10 and 30 points (a slight overshoot is safe); with 100 points 90.3%; with 500, where the correction is negligible, 89.9% either way. The variation between runs, however, stays large when the set is small (a standard deviation of 0.079 with 10 points against 0.014 with 500). The correction is what makes the guarantee finite-sample valid: it holds for any size of calibration set, but a small one gives noisy intervals."
