       "say": "**Average coverage can hide where it fails.** In this data the noise grows with x. A constant-width interval is wide enough on average, but split the test points by x and see: coverage is **100%** for x from 0 to 2, 98% and 89% in the middle and only **72%** for x from 8 to 10. The interval is too wide where the data are quiet and too narrow where they are noisy. The fix is a **normalised score**: divide each error by an estimate of the typical error at that x (a small second model fitted to the calibration errors), then multiply back when predicting. Coverage becomes much more even across the range, **94%, 92%, 89%, 88% and 86%**, and the intervals widen where the noise does. It is still not perfect (the guarantee covers only the average), but the worst region has improved from 72% to 86%.",
